#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c477f-e1fc-50ef-ac57-d12fc1cc570e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{111}^{∘},-{69}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284047} {:answer/id #uuid "83790c99-f1c7-59b6-a48d-a19566b640c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{121}^{∘},-{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284048} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c12e6c-482d-5acf-9314-22e60b87a587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{107}^{∘},-{73}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284049} {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c1f0-6b3c-5110-b0bd-e499554b6908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284050} {:answer/id #uuid "753b1e0a-89ef-55d2-9795-dd1bd3860e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{104}^{∘},-{76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c25c1f0-6b3c-5110-b0bd-e499554b6908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284050} :answer-field/id #uuid "54bc84cc-8580-5aa4-ae4f-b8b92480f2a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8a9c8df-52c0-5718-91ea-887526dc88c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120856" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-8$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-8 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{8}{9})=-62.733\\,955{…}^{∘}≈-{62.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{62.73}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{62.73}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={62.73}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992768/assets/62808ce876d31757e1b46863b014bbf83209c414099ade0f93b91303c6702d1f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{62.73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={242.73}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘}={242.73}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{117.27}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x=-{117}^{∘},-{63}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bab53d09-03d0-54d6-91d2-737ce8986c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284054} {:answer/id #uuid "6602a85f-2f79-56d6-a22c-cfe307b872de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab53d09-03d0-54d6-91d2-737ce8986c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284054} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88af14a-7d05-5aa2-8f96-5bbd53b2249a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "111545ed-4c9e-5cbd-9d4f-964c8b9e9d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-274619" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation  $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$ Give your answer in radians.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d5f124-8e82-5228-8094-c854056d6781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d5f124-8e82-5228-8094-c854056d6781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a614d49-d44e-57f6-b931-f954a27e0ff3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a9afa3-e216-5e40-92e9-8113111cf229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a9afa3-e216-5e40-92e9-8113111cf229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284060} :answer-field/id #uuid "582191eb-2601-5a9b-9f0e-e063123df266" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80542820-66e7-54c9-b568-52fdb299fda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332213" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.85,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.85)=58.211\\,669…≈{58.2117}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince ${58.2117}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={58.2117}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={58.2117}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={58.2117}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992768/assets/ec4366c4e46cc9c1df5fb31aa2ca04f337fd3c6d0bce034405ca2b48874aa32a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{58.2117}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{121.7883}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{58.2}^{∘},\\,x≈{121.8}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a13b1463-1c59-56e8-b437-629427d9552f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284063} {:answer/id #uuid "96273325-53f0-5db0-8880-0214d0be01ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a13b1463-1c59-56e8-b437-629427d9552f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284063} :answer-field/id #uuid "098b7cd1-85e7-596a-be75-81ce8d1b3a7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8a6222a-9921-523a-b786-a8bd4fe09459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a6222a-9921-523a-b786-a8bd4fe09459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284066} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d26eb2-1a9a-556c-bbbe-0d20a5c90d93" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a05f22c-176f-5398-a965-22f34f30c670" :question/math-academy-id "q-332380" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5},$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$  are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})=0.643\\,501…≈0.6435\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.6435$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.6435.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992768/assets/962545fd6fa64e121a99eb8bf684484e4b457dbd4defedd7c07ae88fd47cac6e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.6435 \\\\  & ≈2.4981.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈0.6,\\,x≈2.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e986010a-6cc1-57c9-a203-528ebf9ebda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e986010a-6cc1-57c9-a203-528ebf9ebda1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284069} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1ce512-23c6-5822-bc7b-3afc53262884" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a5703dc-e2a4-51a9-88ec-33d050a3db18" :question/math-academy-id "q-336507" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to the equation  $4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Give your answer in radians.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ does not lie inside the required domain $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ we add one full period of $2\\pi $ to find an equivalent angle in the domain:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThus, our first solution in the required domain is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution."}]}
